I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.' I read 'when any fence holds one' as 'when any code fence contains a JSON object', even if the fence also contains other text. The two replies are: first, the string '```json\n{\"a\": 1}\nextra prose\n```\n{\"b\": 2}'; second, the string '{\"b\": 2}'. For the first reply, the code fence contains the JSON object {\"a\": 1}, so the rule requires the function to return {\"a\": 1} with no duplicates. For the second reply, there is no code fence, so the rule requires the last top-level object, {\"b\": 2}, with no duplicates. The function, however, returns {\"b\": 2} for both: in the first reply the fenced content '{\"a\": 1}\nextra prose' does not parse as a single JSON object, so it is passed over, and the function falls back to the last top-level object {\"b\": 2}. What would make me wrong is if the intended reading of 'when any fence holds one' is that the fenced content must itself be a single scoreable object; under that reading the first reply's fence holds no scoreable object, the rule would also require {\"b\": 2}, and the function would be correct.